BCP admin recipe special-skis. Cut from ski-demo_special-skis_dev_10-19-12_v12.mp4 (375.88s). The screens were MEASURED off that capture — the totals agree with its own length to a tenth of a second — so every line here was written to a screen that already exists and nothing needs stretching. ⚠ THE REPEATED ITEMS ARE SILENT ON PURPOSE. Carson, 2026-09-10: "add the voice for every scene, except for the the items, since they are all repeats of the same first item." The first item is narrated in full and its closing line tells the viewer what the silent stretch is before they watch it; the clones are one scene with no cue, as long as the footage it covers, so every later line stays on its own screen. That is a deliberate exception to standard 1 of the raw-video standard, not an omission. Lines carry {0.3} beat markers; a marker is never counted as a word and its silence is always added to the scene.

EVERY SCENE HAS A LEAD-IN AND AN EXIT PAUSE.

  lead-in  0.5s   Sarah on screen before she starts. A scene that opens
                mid-word has no edit point.
  exit     0.8s   after she stops — the join the next scene's crossfade
                eats into, and what an editor trims to.

  A scene is lead-in + speech + exit. The cue covers ONLY the speech;
  the scene's own start and end are in its NOTE.

THE CUE TIMES ARE THE NARRATION TIMELINE, not the footage. The `raw` line
in each NOTE is how long that screen was really on camera. They differ on
purpose — a screen that takes 15 seconds to fill in does not need 15
seconds of talking over it. What matters is that no screen is SHORTER
than its scene.

